\section{One Description, Two Consumers}

A CPU executes an instruction stream on hardware that already exists. A hardware description can instead specify the hardware that will exist: which interfaces connect, which values are computed in parallel, and which old values survive a clock edge. A simulator executes a model of this description on a host computer. A synthesis tool turns a supported subset into a network of logic and storage cells. These are not interchangeable meanings of ``run.'' Simulation can also execute a testbench that drives clocks, generates stimuli, and prints observations; none of this makes the testbench part of the circuit.

The distinction is visible in Venkataramani, Budiu, Chelcea, and Goldstein's \emph{C to Asynchronous Dataflow Circuits}. Their compiler produces structural Verilog from a dataflow representation, then passes that Verilog to commercial synthesis and place-and-route tools. The Verilog is an intermediate deliverable, not a layout. In \emph{The Vienna Architecture Description Language}, Himmelbauer and colleagues distinguish a processor's ISA from its microarchitecture; they generate both cycle-accurate simulation and synthesizable HDL using microarchitecture information. An instruction's arithmetic meaning alone does not tell us how many pipeline stages its implementation has.

Register-transfer level (RTL) names a useful level of description, not a file format. An RTL author typically chooses the state boundaries and the logic relating each boundary to the next. A structural HDL description can instead instantiate individual gates or library cells, and a behavioral model can omit the implementation structure altogether. A statement valid for simulation at any of these levels is not automatically a recipe for a circuit. For instance, a testbench printing a value every ten nanoseconds says nothing about which device resource would perform the printing.

\Cref{fig:visual-303} separates the artifacts of a conventional digital implementation flow. The fork describes the distinction between the two consumers, not a requirement that both use the same frontend.

