% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Follow a Conditional Register Update}

This Verilog-2005 module uses constructs normally supported by RTL synthesis and has a corresponding event-driven simulation model \cite{ieee1364verilog}. It has no reset; the example's contract begins after an enabled, known input has been sampled.

\begin{verbatim}
module mix_capture (
    input  wire       clk, en,
    input  wire [3:0] a, b,
    output reg  [3:0] q
);
    wire [3:0] mixed;
    assign mixed = a ^ b;
    always @(posedge clk)
        if (en) q <= mixed;
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

For this abstract walkthrough, assume binary inputs that are stable around each capture edge. ``Between edges'' describes a logical relationship, not a guarantee of zero physical propagation time.

\begin{verbatim}
between edges:
    mixed = bitwise_xor(a, b)  # four results, no stored state
at each rising edge:
    next_q = q                 # holding is the default
    if en == 1: next_q = mixed # select what will be captured
    commit(next_q)             # update all four state bits
\end{verbatim}

With \(a=1010_2\), \(b=1100_2\), and \texttt{en} high, the next rising edge captures \(0110_2\). Changing the inputs with \texttt{en} low still changes \texttt{mixed}, but no rising edge with \texttt{en} low can change \texttt{q}. There is no loop over the four bits in the implemented machine. There is one logical XOR per bit, and there are four state bits sampled together. Before the first enabled capture, the four-state simulation starts \texttt{q} at \texttt{x}; no physical power-up value is specified by the module.

\section{Inspect the Handoffs}

Analysis checks syntax and what names mean. Elaboration chooses a top, specializes parameters, and connects instances, yielding a concrete hierarchy. That hierarchy can still contain processes. In synthesis those processes are lowered to combinational logic and state elements. One possible lowering places a mux before each flip-flop and feeds the old output back to one mux input. A target mapper might instead use a cell with a built-in enable. The Verilog says when the old value survives; it does not specify which of those implementations to use. If the enable is proven constant, or if the captured value cannot reach an observable output, later optimization may simplify or remove logic while preserving the selected design's observable behavior.

The \href{https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/latest/using_yosys/synthesis/proc.html}{Yosys process-conversion documentation} describes how its \texttt{proc} pass lowers processes, including mux and register extraction. Reading its output after that pass is useful for checking storage inference, but it is not evidence that the design meets a clock period. Timing closure requires target cells, constraints, and a physical implementation model. Conversely, a trace from an RTL testbench can establish that this particular stimulus produced the expected capture and hold, but it neither measures cell timing nor covers all input sequences.

Each handoff answers a different debugging question. If an enabled capture consistently produces the wrong XOR, start at the RTL boundary and check the bit-level function. If the XOR is correct but the output follows input edits without a rising edge, inspect state inference and how the waveform was sampled. If logical tests pass and an implementation still misses its period, inspect the reported physical paths and the constraints used to analyze them. Turning up the simulated clock rate in a zero-delay RTL test does not reproduce a timing violation; the test model has no propagation delay to exceed.
